<Project>

  <!--
    Central Package Management for EVERY project in SlayIdleRepeat.

    One version per package id, declared once, here. A .csproj names WHAT it
    depends on; this file decides WHICH VERSION, and MSBuild rejects a Version=
    attribute on a PackageReference (NU1008) so the two can never disagree.

    Why it exists: M1-00 needed Shouldly at one version across four test
    projects, and "four csproj files that each happen to say 4.3.0 today" is not
    a pin — it is four opportunities to drift, silently, in four different
    milestones. The same argument had already been made for the assertion
    library it replaced, which was held back from a licence-changing major
    version by nothing but four copies of the same string.

    🔒 This does NOT weaken build/ci/Test-VendorPackageUniqueness.ps1 or
    SlayIdleRepeat.Architecture.Tests' ProjectFileTests. Both read the
    `Include` attribute of `PackageReference` in the .csproj, which CPM leaves
    exactly where it was; only `Version` moves here. The vendor gate even
    carries a vacuity guard naming this migration by name — it fails loudly if
    a future change ever empties the .csproj of references altogether.

    Adding a package: add the PackageVersion here AND the PackageReference in
    the one project that needs it. If it is not a vendor SDK behind a port, it
    also needs an entry in build/ci/non-vendor-packages.json, or A9-UNIQUE goes
    red the moment a second project wants it.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>

    <!--
      A PackageReference that names a package with no PackageVersion here is an
      error, not a silent floating resolve.
    -->
    <CentralPackageVersionOverrideEnabled>false</CentralPackageVersionOverrideEnabled>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Test infrastructure. Shared by every suite under tests/ by construction, and
    allow-listed as non-vendor in build/ci/non-vendor-packages.json for exactly
    that reason.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.14.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit" Version="2.9.3" />
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="3.1.5" />

    <!--
      The assertion library, in all four test suites.

      🔒 Shouldly, 4.3.0, BSD-3-Clause. The library it replaced moved to a paid
      commercial licence at its next major, and this repository sat pinned below
      that version to stay off it — a pin that would have had to hold for the
      life of the project and bought nothing. M1-00 left it instead, while the
      exposure was 561 assertions rather than the several thousand M1-M4 add.
      Do not reintroduce it.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Shouldly" Version="4.3.0" />

    <!--
      The coverage collector, in every suite under tests/ - it is what provides
      the "XPlat code coverage" data collector that coverage.runsettings names.
      Without a reference, a `dotnet test` run pointed at coverage.runsettings
      accepts the file, exits green and writes no coverage at all;
      scripts/Measure-Crap.ps1 fails loudly on that rather than reporting an
      empty ranking.

      6.0.x rather than the 10.x head on purpose: 6.0.4 is the line built for
      VSTest-hosted `dotnet test` on net8.0, which is what Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk
      17.14.1 above gives us. The 8.x and 10.x majors follow the Microsoft.Testing
      .Platform runner this repository does not use. Revisit at whichever milestone
      moves the suites onto that runner, not before.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="coverlet.collector" Version="6.0.4" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Architecture-suite machinery. Not a vendor SDK: these read assemblies, they
    do not talk to a service, so they cannot lock the project in to anything.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageVersion Include="NetArchTest.Rules" Version="1.3.2" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Mono.Cecil" Version="0.11.3" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Logging. Not a vendor SDK: Serilog writes structured JSON to stdout, so the
    log backend is whatever scrapes the container's output — nothing here can
    lock the project in. Referenced by SlayIdleRepeat.Server only, and
    allow-listed in build/ci/non-vendor-packages.json (M5-11).
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageVersion Include="Serilog.AspNetCore" Version="9.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Vendor SDKs. 14 §1.1 🔒: each of these belongs to exactly ONE adapter
    project, and Test-VendorPackageUniqueness.ps1 fails the build if a second
    project references one. Listing a version here does not grant anyone the
    right to reference it — the uniqueness gate reads the .csproj files, not
    this file.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageVersion Include="AWSSDK.S3" Version="4.0.102" />
    <PackageVersion Include="FirebaseAdmin" Version="3.6.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Google.Apis.AndroidPublisher.v3" Version="1.75.0.4221" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Npgsql" Version="10.0.3" />
    <PackageVersion Include="OpenTelemetry.Exporter.OpenTelemetryProtocol" Version="1.17.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="OpenTelemetry.Extensions.Hosting" Version="1.17.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Sentry" Version="6.8.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="StackExchange.Redis" Version="3.1.13" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    🔒 The engine's managed API, pinned to the 4.7.1-stable the export spike ran
    against. SlayIdleRepeat.Adapters.Platform.Godot is a plain SDK project, so it must
    name GodotSharp explicitly and therefore needs a version here. That one reference
    is the whole vendor budget for the engine, and the uniqueness gate above is what
    keeps it there.

    🔴 The condition is not tidiness. SlayIdleRepeat.Client builds on Godot.NET.Sdk,
    which references GodotSharp implicitly, and NuGet fails RESTORE with NU1009 the
    moment a central version exists for an implicitly-referenced package — measured on
    this repository, not guessed, and NoWarn does not reach a restore error. Excluding
    the one project that never names the package by hand is the narrowest fix
    available. Both halves still land on the same bits: Godot.NET.Sdk/4.7.1 injects
    GodotSharp 4.7.1.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition=" '$(MSBuildProjectName)' != 'SlayIdleRepeat.Client' ">
    <PackageVersion Include="GodotSharp" Version="4.7.1" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
